					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-09 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>It&#39;s landmark time, fam...correct me if I&#39;m wrong, but I believe this to be the first ever Mass Line post from the state of Illinois. Feels good, don&#39;t it? 96 degree/6000% humidity weather has me longing for some of that rain. Oh well. On to the craziness...<br /><br />Apple has just dropped a major update to their iMac line, and things are looking very good from where I&#39;m standing. iMacs have long been the weapon of choice for musicians, graphic designers, and movie doods looking to buy into the Apple world without dropping a second mortgage on a Mac Pro, and the recent update has made these things that much more desirable. The base 17&quot; model has gone the way of Rakim&#39;s career, with the 20&quot;/24&quot; joints picking up the slack. The big news here, aside from a Firewire 800 for crazy high-speed data transfer, is the speed increase on the processor side. With up to 2.8 Ghz of Core 2 Duo goodness coupled with support for up to 4 GB of onboard memory, you can load as many Pro Tools plugins, Flash movies, or 15 megapixel Photoshop spreads as your little heart can handle. I&#39;m sold. The new design, on the other hand, looks like a buckled, overgrown iPhone. I am not a fan, which is surprising given my general affinity for Apple&#39;s overall aesthetic. Matter of opinion, I suppose. All told, if you&#39;re in the market for a media powerhouse.....buy. one. now.<br /><br />That said, Blue Scholars are rocking the venerable Knitting Factory in a matter of minutes...drop the credit card, the iMac can wait.<br /><br />Oh so crazy. <br /><br /><br />Budo<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-08 | Wednesday</font></strong><br><br><div style="text-align: center"><img src="http://www.knittingfactory.com/media/fckeditor/bluescholars.jpg" alt="" width="400" height="521" /></div><br /><br /><div align="center"><strong>IT&#39;S GOING DOWN TONIGHT, LOS ANGELES!</strong><br /></div><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-08 | Wednesday</font></strong><br><br>I just finished watching the second <strong>Harry Potter</strong> movie and need to post this up before I knock out for tonight. Today&#39;s DL is a radio interview broadcasted live on <strong>KALX in Berkeley</strong>, 7/17/07. Hosted by <strong>DJ Queen Bee</strong> (big ups!); witness the Scholars attempt to give away free stuff via Seattle trivia (to no avail); management calling in mid-show; stressing the importance of an all-ages show; shout-outs to some massline forum heads; and <strong>Bayani</strong> tracks scattered inbetween. We&#39;re also treated to some <strong>Common Market</strong> instrumentals as well. Holler at the link and hopefully it&#39;ll get you through that all nighter, or your boring work day, your afternoon workout...you get the point. By the way, <strong>Ron Weasley</strong> is my dude...and I agree with <strong>Khingz</strong> in his comparisons to Ron and <strong>Richard Pryor</strong> (facial-expression wise, if you&#39;ve ever seen <em>Toy</em> or <em>Brewster&#39;s Millions</em>, that is).<br /><br /><strong>Blue Scholars - Live on KALX in Berkeley:</strong><br />http://www.zshare.net/download/30204964e58430/<br /><br /><strong>Tracklisting:</strong><br />Soul Position - Hand Me Downs &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Aloe Blacc - I&#39;m Beautiful (Instro) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Blue Scholars - Bayani &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Blue Scholars - Loyalty &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Halogen Majesty - New Testament<br />Kid 606 - Good Times &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;<br />Air - Mer du Japon &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Blue Scholars - North By Northwest &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Fabulous 3 MCs - Rub-A-Dub-Dub &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Lifesavas &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; - Gutterfly &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Blue Scholars - 50K Deep &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; <br />Common Market - Connect For (Instro) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Blue Scholars - Second Chapter &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Busdriver &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; - Unemployed Black Astronaut &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Bass Nectar - Select Frequency &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Snakes Say Hisss! - Take It Slow &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />Quintron and Miss Pussycat - Witch In The Club &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <br />PSD the Drivah - Cus, Cus (Instro) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp; <br />Hollertronix - Don&#39;t Go Snitchen&#39; <br /><br />Peace<br />daps<br /> <p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-07 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">The new <strong>Flight of the Conchords</strong> EP &quot;<strong><em>The Distant Future</em></strong>,&quot; drops today on Seattle&#39;s own <strong>Sub Pop Records</strong>. I got hip to their HBO show about 4 episodes into the season and I gotta say - shit is genius! Have you seen <span style="font-weight: bold" class="Apple-style-span">Hari</span> &amp; <span style="font-weight: bold" class="Apple-style-span">Zia&#39;s</span> short film &quot;<span style="font-style: italic" class="Apple-style-span">Manoj</span>&quot; (check for it at a Massline show or at several upcoming film festivals) yet? See if you can find the tiny&nbsp;<span style="font-weight: bold" class="Apple-style-span">Arj Barker</span>&nbsp;cameo (who plays &quot;Dave&quot; on the show - you&#39;re a legend, Dave).&nbsp;</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">Speaking of Indians, big up to Hari and <strong>Saba</strong> for the last blog entry. Something about New York makes you want to tell people what you did there (remember Saba&#39;s NYC fashion blog)?</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; min-height: 13px; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">And now, we&#39;re off to hook up with the good folks at <strong>Pnoy Apparel</strong> here in San Diego. 619 where you at!</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">&nbsp;</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">Peace</p><p style="font: normal normal normal 11px/normal Verdana; margin: 0px">Geo</p><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2007-08-07 | Tuesday</font></strong><br><br><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/flush.jpg" alt="" /><br />nyc foto jurnelle #2.  by hari and saba<br /><br />Friday, we went on a little tour of <span style="font-weight: bold">Queens</span>, the birthplace of our friend <span style="font-weight: bold">Hari Kondabolu</span>. We met Hari in <span style="font-weight: bold">Flushing</span>, off the infamous <span style="font-weight: bold">7 train</span> that baseball player/ racist <span style="font-weight: bold">John Rocker </span>once railed against as an example of all the things he hated about NYC. <br /><br />NOTE: Blue Scholars and Hari Kondabolu LOVE the 7 train. <br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic1.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Hari is wearing a shirt that says &ldquo;India.&rdquo; We found this redundant. (It is, in fact, a replica of the Indian National Team&rsquo;s cricket jersey.)<br /><br />We agreed we had 3 things to accomplish on our trip:<br /><br />1)    Find out who makes the best <span style="font-weight: bold">Masala Dosa</span> in Queens.<br />2)    Find the best <span style="font-weight: bold">Burfi</span> in Queens.<br />3)    Find a bottle of <span style="font-weight: bold">Mazaa Mango Juice</span>.<br /><br />We began our journey with a walk to the famous <span style="font-weight: bold">Hindu Temple</span> in Flushing. Though it was 50,000 degrees outside, Saba insisted we walk to so Blue Scholars could properly acquaint themselves with Queens.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic2.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />The temple, a beautiful structure completed in 1977, is devoted to <span style="font-weight: bold">Lord Ganesha</span>.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic3.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />We were not allowed to take pictures inside the temple, so we watched Hari pray. (He&rsquo;s very good at praying.)<br /><br />Here is the prayer proof that Hari went to the temple:<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic4.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Onto our first question: Who had the finest Masala Dosa in Queens?     <br /><br />To save time, Hari narrowed the choices between the <span style="font-weight: bold">Temple Canteen</span>, in the basement of the Ganesha temple, and the <span style="font-weight: bold">Dosa Hutt</span>&hellip;which, conveniently enough, was right next door to the temple.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline">First: </span><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration: underline">THE CANTEEN.</span><br /><br />Geo, being the obvious fan of kitsch Americana he is, noted that he&rsquo;d likely think the Canteen would be far superior to anything called a &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">hut</span>.&rdquo;  You see, years of colonialism will do this to your cultural identity.  What do you think of when you hear the word &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">hut</span>?&rdquo;  Island people, obviously, because you&rsquo;re probably a <span style="font-weight: bold">racist</span>.  (For more details on racists, see Wikipedia) &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">Canteen</span>&rdquo; on the other hand, clearly invokes images of John Wayne and the Wild, Wild West.  Thus, the inferiority complex shared by victims of colonialism and their desire to imitate their oppressors, needless to say, will make a normal person of color reject their inherent desire to sit in a &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">hut</span>&rdquo; in favor of throwin a few back in a &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">canteen</span>.&rdquo; <br /><br />So sad. (Hari would like to note that he did not<span style="font-style: italic"> </span>not write the previous paragraph.)<br /><br />Getting to the Canteen, however, would prove confusing&hellip; well maybe it was more confusing how over-informative the &ldquo;<span style="font-weight: bold">signs-every-five-feet</span>&rdquo; directions were.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic5.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />But eventually&hellip;HAZAA!  Unfortunately for our young rapper, no oversized cowboy hats, no sasparilla, no unruly poker games with players carrying Derringers ready to snap, no honky-tonk piano.  Sigh, just raagas.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic6.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />We checked the menu and ordered a <span style="font-weight: bold">Masala Dosa</span>.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic7.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Now, some of you might be asking at this point, &ldquo;You&rsquo;re at a Hindu temple, why is it that the relations between the world&rsquo;s religious communities are so filled with strife?&rdquo;  That is a good question, friend.  A more appropriate question at this point in the story however, would be &ldquo;what is dosa?&rdquo;  Let&rsquo;s answer that.<br /><br />The <span style="font-style: italic">dose</span>, <span style="font-style: italic; font-weight: bold">dosa</span>, <span style="font-style: italic">dosai</span>, <span style="font-style: italic">thosai</span>, or <span style="font-style: italic">dhosa</span> is a South Indian dish.  For the folks who understand things best in <span style="font-weight: bold">Eurocentric terms</span>, think of it as an <span style="font-weight: bold">Indian cr&ecirc;pe</span>.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic8.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />According to Wikipedia, regular dosa batter is made from <span style="font-weight: bold">lentils and rice</span> blended with water and left to ferment overnight.  The batter is then ladled in small amounts onto a hot greased skillet, where it is spread out into a thin circle and fried with <span style="font-weight: bold">oil</span> or <span style="font-weight: bold">ghee</span> until golden brown. The dosa may then be folded in half and served, or it may be flipped to cook on the other side and then served.<br /><br />We ordered a dosa with potato curry in the middle (Masala Dosa) and chutney on the side.  We then concluded that these would be the best categories with which to judge our meals:  Curry, Dosa, Chutney.<br /><br />The dosa at the canteen was spicy. However, Hari felt it was &ldquo;too spicy.&rdquo; He added further that he felt the spice was covering up what seemed to be a less than spectacular cuisine. What are you trying to hide, Temple Canteen?? Geo and Saba concurred with this assessment and felt the coconut chutney was also too spicy. Overall, however, pretty solid.<br /><br />Hari did point out that he disapproved of the Temple&rsquo;s use of <span style="font-weight: bold">Styrofoam</span>, particularly the color of the Styrofoam...  Perhaps this was a Canteen afterall.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline">Next up: </span><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration: underline">DOSA HUTT</span><span style="text-decoration: underline">.</span><br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic9.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />In addition to our order of Masala Dosa, Hari ordered a side of <span style="font-weight: bold">idli</span> (no spell-check, we did not mean &ldquo;idly,&rdquo; we meant <span style="font-weight: bold">idli</span>- the South Indian rice cakes) and a bottle of <span style="font-weight: bold">Thums Up</span>.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic10.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br /><span style="font-weight: bold">Thums Up</span> is the <span style="font-weight: bold">Indian version of Coca-Cola</span>, even more so now that Coca-Cola owns the company. We enjoyed this beverage, especially because of the logo of a red thumb being bent farther than humanly possible. We wondered if there was some communist meaning to this that we were unaware of. The hand of the worker being forced to appreciate soda more than is humanly possible...?<br /><br />Saba also noticed a very disturbing sign that he felt encouraged choking victims.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic11.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />After eating the dosa at Dosa Hutt, we had a clear victor. The Dosa Hutt dosa and curry were less spicy, but had far more flavor. The same was true with the chutney which we also all liked because it was less spicy and green instead of white. Hari, however, was once again disappointed by the use of <span style="font-style: italic">white </span>Styrofoam.  <br /><br />We concluded the fact that the commercial enterprise, whose dosa was 25 cents cheaper than the temple&rsquo;s, had won this contest was proof that capitalism really CAN work. Sike!<br /><br />Next, we took a bus back to Flushing and then the 7 train to where Hari grew up until he was 9: A part of <span style="font-weight: bold">Jackson Heights</span> known as <span style="font-weight: bold">Little India</span>. We still had to find this mango juice Hari raved about and have our burfi contest.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic12.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />We first searched unsuccessfully for <span style="font-weight: bold">Mazaa Mango Juice</span> at <span style="font-weight: bold">Patel Brothers</span>, the largest South Asian supermarket in the United States. We then wandered around Little India and saw movie theatres, jewelry shops, sari stores (with mannequins that looked nothing like the darker skinned people of the subcontinent who generally purchase saris) and music shops. In truth, the area is also home of many <span style="font-weight: bold">Pakistani</span> and <span style="font-weight: bold">Bangladeshi </span>immigrants, and is a great indicator of the Indian subcontinent&rsquo;s great diversity--something that the term &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">Indian subcontinent</span>&rdquo; itself does not do justice.<br /><br />Saba noted, in addition, that the use of &ldquo;sub&rdquo; as a prefix was never really a good thing anyway, as it always implied inferiority.  Except, of course, in the case of <span style="font-weight: bold">Sub-Zero</span> from <span style="font-weight: bold">Mortal Kombat</span>.  He was undeniably awesome.<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic13.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Then it was time for <span style="font-weight: bold">burfi</span>.<br /><br />According the Wikipedia, burfi or <span style="font-style: italic">barfi</span> (Indian-American children avoid this latter spelling because it has historically lead to ridicule in school) is an Indian sweet &ldquo;made from condensed milk, cooked with sugar until it solidifies.&rdquo; It has been called &ldquo;Indian cheese cake.&rdquo; By who?  Eurocentrists!!! <br /><br />Hari then asked Saba, &ldquo;Hey man, why does Wikipedia think it knows so much about Indian culture?&rdquo;<br /><br />DJ Sabzi of Blue Scholars replied: &ldquo; Well Hari, Wikipedia took a trip to India when it was 22 with some bro&rsquo;s to go &lsquo;find itself&rsquo; like some sort of spiritual Marco Polo. And it did&hellip;along with a bunch of information about Indian food.&rdquo;<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic14.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />First, off to <span style="font-weight: bold">Delhi Palace Sweets</span>, where a very nice young woman served us a piece of <span style="font-weight: bold">plain burfi</span>.  Geo quoted a short verse from a dancehall tune and said &ldquo;<span style="font-style: italic">Eddie Burfi!</span>&rdquo;  Although the burfi here was quite good, we felt we could do better.<br /><br />We then went to <span style="font-weight: bold">Al-Naimat Restaurant and Streets</span>, Hari&rsquo;s favorite sweets spot in Jackson Heights. <br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pic15.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />We purchased some of their fine masala tea and a &ldquo;milk burfi.&rdquo; Geo discovered that burfi was similar to the Filipino dish, polvoron. We all had a nice inter-cultural-laugh about this.  <br /><br />NOTE:The <span style="font-weight: bold">inter-cultural-laugh</span>, a laugh coming from genuine appreciation and respect, is not to be confused with the <span style="font-weight: bold">multi-cultural-laugh</span>, which usually occurs in corporate office meetings and is very awkward.  <br /><br />Geo also discovered that milk burfi is most filthy when held on the tongue for several seconds before hot masala tea washes it away.  This experience put him in much better spirits after the disappointment of the Canteen.<br /><br />After two sweet shops, we were full and decided to call the contest it a draw.<br /><br />We then had a couple of slices of pizza at Hari&rsquo;s childhood pizzeria, the non-South Asian run, <span style="font-weight: bold">Pizza Boy I</span>. <br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/pizzaboy1.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />That&rsquo;s right. Pizzaboy... I. Though it is unclear to the consumer if there is a second Pizzaboy, they gave this restaurant &ldquo;Part I&rdquo; denotation. <br /><br />NOTE: Blue Scholars will now be known as <span style="font-style: italic">Blue Scholars I</span>.<br /><br />Each slice was very greasy and served within minutes and&hellip; TASTED BETTER THAN ANY PIZZA IN ALL OF SEATTLE.<br /><br />(Hari would like to note that he <span style="font-style: italic">did</span> write the previous sentence.)<br /><br />Hari spoke of his childhood memories eating Pizzaboy I pizza on his way back from his elementary school, the inappropriately named <span style="font-weight: bold">P.S. 69</span>.  Geo and Saba laughed.  Hari also laughed but clearly did not understand why.<br /><br />Finally, we were off to complete our search for Mazaa Mango Juice.<br /><br />Hari&rsquo;s initial goal was to find Mazaa Mango juice and introduce it to the Blue Scholars in order to convince the folks at <span style="font-weight: bold">Hidmo</span> to quit <span style="font-style: italic">Jumex and switch over to Hari&rsquo;s favorite brand</span>. He was thinking about the best interests of the greater Seattle community.<br /><br />However, after several hours of searching, this became less about Seattle and more about finding a product that had become integral with his Jackson Heights experience. He forced us to look in every grocery store in the area. We kept coming up with results like this:<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/juice2.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />Two Guavas and a Lychee! No Mango juice?<br /><br />Hari was a man-possessed. And Blue Scholars were bored. We finally gave up the search and bid Hari adieu. <br /><br />However, this story has a post-script. When Hari returned home that day, he would open his refrigerator and discover this:<br /><br /><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/juice1.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br />His mother had purchased him what he desired more than any other earthly possession!<br /><br />Hari then had a crazy <span style="font-weight: bold">Mazaa Mango Juice party</span>.<br /><div style="text-align: left"><br /></div><img src="http://www.masslinemedia.com/images/queens/homies.jpg" alt="" /><br /><br /><span style="font-weight: bold">THE END</span><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><center><div class="pagenumbers"><A HREF="archive.php?from=100">[&lt;&lt;]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=0">[1]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=5">[2]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=10">[3]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=15">[4]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20">[5]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=25">[6]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=30">[7]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=35">[8]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=40">[9]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=45">[10]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=50">[11]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=55">[12]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=60">[13]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=65">[14]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=70">[15]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=75">[16]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=80">[17]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=85">[18]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=90">[19]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[20]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=100">[21]</A> [22] <A HREF="archive.php?from=110">[23]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=115">[24]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=120">[25]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=125">[26]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=130">[27]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=135">[28]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=140">[29]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=145">[30]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=150">[31]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=155">[32]</A> <A HREF